body:
I'll be honest that this idea here felt somewhat off from the beginning and I couldn't really put my finger on it. But I now see that the original proposal is just a more elegant way of solving this issue without introducing any new things into the language.

I feel like using the annotation makes it hard to understand the flow of the code. Looking at the example on the OP, the repetition of the functions looks cluttered and it's hard to make out which is which.

Another issue I see in the test case:

```gdscript
@if_features("test_feature_1", "test_feature_2")
func _various():
	print("other 1")

@if_features("test_feature_1", "test_feature_2")
func _various():
	print("other 1 again")
```

The repetition of features is not an error and only one of these will be called (the first one in the text). If those are separated by many other versions, you could easily be baffled and don't understand why it's not being called. Imagine if you add another case without realizing there was already another one with the same function.

OTOH, the proposal uses existing natural language concepts to achieve the same end goal. For instance, compare with this version:

```gdscript
func _ready():
    if OS.has_feature("windows"):
        _windows_implementation()
    elif OS.has_feature("linuxbsd"):
        if OS.has_feature("s3tc"):
            _linux_s3tc_timplementation()
        elif OS.has_feature("astc"):
            _linux_astc_implementation()
	else:
		_other_implementation()

func _other_implementation():
	print("Other OS...")

func _windows_implementation():
	print("Windows!")

func _linux_s3tc_timplementation():
	print("Linuxxx with S3TC texture compression!")

func _linux_astc_implementation():
	print("Linuxxx with ASTC texture compression!")
```

In this, the code itself tells the flow of the program. There's no guess to which `_implementation()` function will be called. The user will benefit even if they aren't aware of the concept. While with this one the behavior isn't exactly the same (the `_other_implementation()` isn't called if it's on Linux but don't have S3TC nor ASTC), this fact is much more obvious to understand than with the annotations. That is: it's much easier to tell what's _missing_ from the code. While you could, of course, mistakenly add a repeated case here too without any error, since the cases are syntactically contained it's easier to see where the mistake is. If we do implement a way to error out repeated cases, all `if` blocks will benefit from it.

For instance, if you have another function annotated with `@if_features("linuxbsd")` at the beginning, will this one be used if there are other more specific ones? I would expect so, but then if you see this at the beginning you have to scan the rest of the text to see if there are more specific versions that would be called instead. Or perhaps both will be present since both matches the features and then you have a compiler error (and if you do want a fallback in this case, you'd have to be exhaustive in the other feature so all cases are covered).

As I understand from the implementation, only the first will be called and not the more specific one and you can have repeats without error, which would trip me up for sure. That makes it quite complicated (if not impossible) to have default cases per platform.

The downside of this sample I put is naturally that all functions would be present in the export, since there's no information to remove them (we _could_ remove unused function on export but that's a whole new discussions with its own complications). One way to solve this would be concentrate the switches in a single place:

```gdscript
func _ready():
	_implementation()

func _implementation():
    if OS.has_feature("windows"):
        print("Windows!")
    elif OS.has_feature("linuxbsd"):
        if OS.has_feature("s3tc"):
            print("Linuxxx with S3TC texture compression!")
        elif OS.has_feature("astc"):
            print("Linuxxx with ASTC texture compression!")
    else:
        print("Other OS...")
```

Another way would be to repeat the `if` check at the beginning of each function. While repetitive and not intuitive to write, it is clearer to read (with the cost of an extra indentation) and since those are processed at compile time they wouldn't introduce any extra runtime cost.

Another benefit is that we could introduce other kinds of check without much extra cost. For example: `Engine.is_editor_hint()` could be ignored when playing the project and completely stripped at export (without having to add new OS features). Some less obvious too like: `Engine.has_singleton()` and `ClassDB.has_class()` which are used often for plugins/extensions that are not present on all platforms (e.g. mobile APIs) (those are not possible to strip on export without extra information, but at least they would work with zero runtime cost).

In particular for the `has_class()` case, we could completely ignore the block during analysis when it's `false` and use actual static types without failing in the editor:

```gdscript
func _ready():
    # Current:
    if ClassDB.has_class("MobilePlugin"):
        var plugin := ClassDB.instantiate("MobilePluign") # Typo not caught until runtime.
        
    # New:
    if ClassDB.has_class("MobilePlugin"):
        var plugin := MobilePluign.new() # Typo caught at load time.
```

If we want to be extra smart we could evaluate the block in the editor as if the class exists and show the error while the user is still writing code. But this is also another discussion.

----

Now, while that seems way more complicated to implement, I don't think it actually is that complicated. I have an idea in mind on how to achieve this effect. Perhaps we can discuss if you are interested @RandomShaper (feel free to ping me on the chat). Or I'll try to do a basic implementation soon

> But we don't need to go that far. If you are putting other things in the condition that cannot be evaluated at compile time, the condition simply won't be evaluated at compile time. There will be no trimming nor skipping analysis in this case. It will be treated as any other dynamic conditional block.

This is exactly what I mean when I say that implementation magic would cause problems. Because if we avoid stripping code based on a technicality, the user won't understand why the code isn't being stripped, and why it's still evaluating `android_sdk_function_only_defined_for_android()` and throwing an error.

> And I think most people can see that this cannot be evaluated at compile time and would not expect it to be.

It's trivial for a maintainer to make a change to the `if` statement in the code, adding another clause to it, and causing the code stripping to break without realizing what's going on. That's why I'm trying to push for having the function/annotation be decorated specifically, so that the user knows that `OS.has_feature()` is being treated differently by the script interpreter. It doesn't matter whether that logic is inside `OS.has_feature()` or `if` parsing logic, it's still a special case that could trip users up.

Edit: Here's a trivial case that would cause the code stripping to not behave as expected:

```gdscript
var is_initialized: bool = false;

func initialize():
    # Setup logic
    is_initialized = true;

func do_foo():
    if is_initialized && OS.has_feature("android"):
        android_sdk_function_only_defined_for_android();
```

This can especially be an issue with multi-person projects where more than one person is contributing to the code. If one person doesn't know what `OS.has_feature()` is doing behind the scenes (especially because it looks like a regular function), they might add the `is_initialized` check to the same `if` statement and cause the stripping to break. Then another maintainer, who isn't working on Android, suddenly wonders why the code broke for them. They look and see that `OS.has_feature("android")` is still there. The documentation says it will strip the code. But they're unaware of this corner case. If the function were marked specially, a lot of this hassle can probably be avoided.

So I think most of us can agree that the logic in this PR for deciding which overloaded function to use is confusing and error-prone. So here's my proposal.

When using `@if_features`, a function may only be defined **two times**: first, for when all the features are available, and second, for a default fallback. That is, this code should throw a script error:

```gdscript
@if_features("windows")
func _implementation():
    print("Windows!")

@if_features("linuxbsd", "s3tc")
func _implementation():
    print("Linuxxx!")
```

And this should also error, because the second definition did not use the appropriate annotation:

```gdscript
@if_features("windows")
func _implementation():
    print("Windows!")

func _implementation():
    print("Error! Non-annotated default!")
```

The idea is to push users toward writing code like this:

```gdscript
@if_features("editor")
func _implementation():
    print("Running in editor!")
@else
func _implementation():
    print("Not running in editor!")
```

The `_implementation()` in `@else` is used if the feature check for "editor" fails. Alternatively, if you don't need your fallback implementation to do anything, it would simply be:

```gdscript
@if_features("editor")
func _implementation():
    print("Running in editor!")
```

And it would generate an empty fallback function (so that the function is still defined).

You might be thinking that this sort of thing would only work for "binary on/off" features like "editor". Well, you can use it for other things too. When used in combination with `OS.has_feature()`:

```gdscript
extends Node2D

func _ready():
    _implementation()

@if_features("windows")
func _windows_implementation():
    print("Windows!")

@if_features("linuxbsd", "s3tc")
func _linux_s3tc_implementation():
    print("Linuxxx with S3TC texture compression!")

@if_features("linuxbsd", "astc")
func _linux_astc_implementation():
    print("Linuxxx with S3TC texture compression!")

func _implementation():
    if OS.has_feature("windows"):
        _windows_implementation()
    elif OS.has_feature("linuxbsd"):
        if OS.has_feature("s3tc"):
            _linux_s3tc_implementation()
        elif OS.has_feature("astc"):
            _linux_astc_implementation()
    else:
        print("Other OS...")
```

So this fulfills both goals, IMO. It's more lines of code now, but the flow of the if logic is easy to see, and we still have explicit control over when the code is stripped.

I do have one remaining hangup though. The use of an implicit, empty fallback function that does nothing could still trip some people up. We could either force people to explicitly define the fallback function via `@else`, or we could have the default implementation throw a script error when it gets called.

I'm also unsure if we should have the user type out `func _implementation():` twice. We could probably shorten the third code snippet above to:

```gdscript
@if_features("editor")
func _implementation():
    print("Running in editor!")
@else
    print("Not running in editor!")
```

The problem I see though is that `@else` starts to look like a line-level annotation instead of a function annotation. Syntactically speaking it wouldn't make much sense, even if it does help push the user toward the paradigm I'm describing.

following this idea, sounds to me this proposal could have `@elif_features(...)` and `@endif_features` annotations, making it very obvious what is inside each block, so the content of the annotation is no longer a single function, but potentially a couple of them

```gdscript
extends Node2D

func _ready():
    _implementation()

@if_features("windows")
func _implementation():
    print("Windows!")
@elif_features("linuxbsd", "s3tc")
func _implementation():
    print("Linuxxx with S3TC texture compression!")
@elif_features("linuxbsd", "astc")
func _implementation():
    print("Linuxxx with S3TC texture compression!")
@else
func _implementation():
    print("We using ", OS.name)
@endif_features
```

This makes more obvious and intentional what is the order of preference of the author of which version of the code to apply

i dont think we should force implementation in the else, this is only to stript the right part of the code in the right condition, imagine this scenario
